If $r \le 2n$, then we can put at most 2 chess pieces in the $C, D, I, J$-diagonals. By the pigeonhole principle, one chess piece must occupy at least two such diagonals. WLOG assume there is a chess piece in $(1, k+1)$ occupying the $C, D$-diagonals. In order to occupy the $I, J$-diagonals simultaneously, there must be another chess piece in $(2k+1, k+1)$. But then there is no chess piece in row $k+1$. This is a contradiction. Thus, we have $r \ge 2n+1$.

Now, we put $2n + 1$ chess pieces in the following cells:
