After four moves, the numbers on the blackboard can be eight consecutive numbers. Since each move increases the sum by $9$, and the sum of $1$ to $8$ is $36$, four moves are sufficient.

b) After $k$ moves, the sum of the eight numbers is $9k$. Since $8 \cdot 2015 = 16120$ is not a multiple of $9$, the answer is negative.' WHERE id = 5373;
UPDATE public.questoes SET solucao = 'Suppose a prime $p$ satisfies $p^a \mid m$. Then from the given conditions, there exists an infinite subset $A_1$ of $A$ such that $p$ is coprime to every element in $A_1$.
By the pigeonhole principle, there is an infinite subset $A_2$ of $A_1$, such that $x \equiv a \pmod{mn}$, for each element $x \in A_2$, where $a$ is a positive integer and $p \nmid a$.
Since $(m, n) = 1$, we have $(p^a, \frac{mn}{p^a}) = 1$. By the Chinese Remainder Theorem, we know that
$$
\begin{cases} x \equiv a^{-1} \pmod{p^a}, \\ x \equiv 0 \pmod{\frac{mn}{p^a}} \end{cases} \quad ①
$$
have infinitely many solutions. Among them we take one as $x$. Next define $B_p$ as the set of the first $x$ elements in $A_2$, and $S_p$ as the sum of all elements in $B_p$. Then we have $S_p \equiv a x \pmod{mn}$. By ① we have
$$
S_p \equiv a x \equiv 1 \pmod{p^a}, \quad S_p \equiv 0 \pmod{\frac{mn}{p^a}}.
$$

Suppose that $m = p_1^{a_1} \cdots p_k^{a_k}$, and for every $p_i$ ($1 \le i \le k$) select a finite subset $B_i$ of $A$, where $B_i \subset A \setminus (B_1 \cup \cdots \cup B_{i-1})$, such that $S_{p_i}$, the sum of all the elements in $B_i$, satisfies
$$
S_{p_i} \equiv 1 \pmod{p_i^{a_i}}, \quad S_{p_i} \equiv 0 \pmod{\frac{mn}{p_i^{a_i}}}. \qquad \textcircled{2}
$$
Let $B = \bigcup_{i=1}^k B_i$, whose sum of elements then satisfies $S = \sum_{i=1}^k S_i$. According to ②, we have $S \equiv 1 \pmod{p_i^{a_i}}$ ($1 \le i \le k$), and $S \equiv 0 \pmod{n}$. So $B$ is the required subset, and the proof is complete.
Solution 2:

Divide every element in $A$ by $mn$, and let the remainders which occur infinitely be (in order) $\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_k$. We claim that
$$
(\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_k, m) = 1. \qquad \textcircled{3}
$$
Otherwise, assume that $p \mid (\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_k, m)$. Then $p \nmid n$, since $(m,n) = 1$. By the given conditions we know that there exist infinitely many elements of $A$ not divisible by $p$. But by the definition of $\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_k$, the number of such elements are finite. Then by contradiction, ③ holds. Consequently, there are $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_k, y$, satisfying $\alpha_1 x_1 + \alpha_2 x_2 + \dots + \alpha_k x_k - y m = 1$. Choose a suitable positive integer $r$ such that $r n \equiv 1 \pmod{m}$. Then
$$
\alpha_1(r n x_1) + \alpha_2(r n x_2) + \dots + \alpha_k(r n x_k) = r n + r m n y.
$$
We select in order $r x_i$ elements from $A$ such that the remainders by $mn$ are $\alpha_i$ ($i = 1, 2, \dots, k$). The set of all these elements is acquired.' WHERE id = 5377;
UPDATE public.questoes SET solucao = 'Prove that there exists even a collection of such $b_i$''s such that each $b_i/b_j$ is a power of $2$. For this purpose, for each $i$ choose such collection with $b_i = a_i$ and minimal possible $b_j$''s with $j \neq i$. The product of all $n^2$ numbers in these $n$ collections is at most $(2^{(n-1)/2} a_1 a_2 \cdots a_n)^n$.
Мы докажем, что существуют даже числа $b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n$, удовлетворяющие следующим (более сильным) условиям:
(1) $b_i \ge a_i$ при всех $i \le n$;
(2) $b_1b_2\dots b_n \le 2^{(n-1)/2} a_1a_2\dots a_n$;
(3) отношение любых двух из чисел $b_i$ является степенью двойки (с целым показателем).

Заметим, что доказываемое утверждение не изменится, если какое-то из чисел $a_k$ (а с ним и соответствующее $b_k$) умножить на некоторую степень двойки. Умножим каждое из чисел $a_k$ на степень двойки так, чтобы все полученные числа лежали в промежутке $[1, 2)$.
Не умаляя общности можно считать, что $1 \le a_1 \le a_2 \le \dots \le a_n < 2$. Покажем теперь, что одна из следующих $n$ последовательностей удовлетворяет всем трём условиям:

$$
\begin{align*}
a_1, & \quad a_1, \quad 2a_1, \quad 2a_1, \quad \dots, \quad 2a_1, \quad 2a_1; \\
a_2, & \quad a_2, \quad 2a_2, \quad 2a_2, \quad \dots, \quad 2a_2, \quad 2a_2; \\
a_3, & \quad a_3, \quad a_3, \quad 2a_3, \quad \dots, \quad 2a_3, \quad 2a_3; \\
& \dots \\
a_{n-1}, & \quad a_{n-1}, \quad a_{n-1}, \quad a_{n-1}, \quad \dots, \quad a_{n-1}, \quad 2a_{n-1}; \\
a_n, & \quad a_n, \quad a_n, \quad a_n, \quad \dots, \quad a_n, \quad a_n.
\end{align*}
$$

Поскольку для любых $k$ и $l$ выполнено неравенство $2a_l \ge 2 > a_k$, каждая из последовательностей удовлетворяет (1). Кроме того, каждая из последовательностей, очевидно, удовлетворяет (3).
Осталось показать, что хотя бы одна из них удовлетворяет (2).
Для этого заметим, что произведение всех $n^2$ чисел во всех $n$ последовательностях равно
$$
2^{(n-1)+(n-2)+\dots+0} \cdot a_1^n a_2^n \dots a_n^n = (2^{(n-1)/2} a_1 a_2 \dots a_n)^n .
$$
Следовательно, произведение чисел хотя бы в одной из последовательностей не превосходит $2^{(n-1)/2} a_1 a_2 \dots a_n$, что и требовалось.' WHERE id = 5387;
UPDATE public.questoes SET solucao = 'We denote the angle between the planes $AND$ and $ACD$ by $\alpha$, and the angle between the planes $AMD$ and $AND$ by $\beta$. Furthermore, when $P, Q, R$ and $S$ are four points in space which do not all lie on a plane, we denote the volume of the tetrahedron $PQRS$ by $|PQRS|$.
The triangles $\triangle MBD$, $\triangle MCD$, $\triangle NBD$ and $\triangle NCD$ have a common height, which is the distance of the point $D$ from the line $BC$. Therefore
$$
\frac{MB}{MC} + \frac{NB}{NC} = \frac{|\triangle MBD|}{|\triangle MCD|} + \frac{|\triangle NBD|}{|\triangle NCD|}.
$$
In the same vein, since the points $M, B, C$ and $D$ lie on a common plane, which does not contain the point $A$, we must have
$$
\frac{|\triangle MBD|}{|\triangle MCD|} = \frac{|MBDA|}{|MCDA|}.
$$
Similarly,
$$
\frac{|\triangle NBD|}{|\triangle NCD|} = \frac{|NBDA|}{|NCDA|}.
$$
Let us consider the volume of the tetrahedron $MBDA$. Naturally
$$
|MBDA| = \frac{1}{3} \cdot |\triangle ABD| \cdot h,
$$
where $h$ is the distance of $M$ from the plane $ABD$. Let $P$ be the projection of $M$ to the line $AD$ and let $Q$ be the projection of $M$ to the plane $ABD$. Then
$$
h = MQ, \quad \angle QPM = \alpha, \quad \text{and} \quad |\triangle AMD| = \frac{1}{2} \cdot AD \cdot PM.
$$
Because in addition $h = PM \cdot \sin \alpha$, we reach the conclusion
$$
|MBDA| = \frac{2 \cdot |\triangle ABD| \cdot |\triangle AMD| \sin \alpha}{3 \cdot AD}.
$$

$\frac{MB}{MC} + \frac{NB}{NC} = \frac{|MBDA|}{|MCDA|} + \frac{|NBDA|}{|NCDA|} \ge 2 \sqrt{\frac{|MBDA| \cdot |NBDA|}{|MCDA| \cdot |NCDA|}} = 2 \cdot \sqrt{\frac{\frac{2 \cdot |\triangle ABD| \cdot |\triangle AMD| \sin \alpha}{3 \cdot AD} \cdot \frac{2 \cdot |\triangle ABD| \cdot |\triangle AND| \sin (\alpha + \beta)}{3 \cdot AD}}{\frac{2 \cdot |\triangle ACD| \cdot |\triangle AMD| \sin (\alpha + \beta)}{3 \cdot AD} \cdot \frac{2 \cdot |\triangle ACD| \cdot |\triangle AND| \sin \alpha}{3 \cdot AD}}} = 2 \cdot \frac{|\triangle ABD|}{|\triangle ACD|}$, (prepared by the Organizing Committee)
Let $B''$ be a point so that $BB'' \parallel AD$ and $B''C \perp AD$. Let $M'', N''$ be points on $B''C$ so that $MM'' \parallel NN'' \parallel BB''$. Let $E$ be a point on $AD$ such that the plane $B''CE$ is perpendicular to $AD$. Then $\frac{MB}{MC} = \frac{M''B''}{M''C}$, $\frac{NB}{NC} = \frac{N''B''}{N''C}$, and $\frac{|\Delta ABD|}{|\Delta ACD|} = \frac{|\Delta AB''D|}{|\Delta ACD|} = \frac{B''E}{CE}$.
Since the angle between planes $AND$, $ACD$ is equal to the angle between planes $AMD$, $ABD$, it follows that lines $EM''$, $EN''$ are isogonal in the angle $B''EC$. It is well-known that $\frac{M''B''}{M''C} \cdot \frac{N''B''}{N''C} = \frac{{B''E}^2}{CE^2}$. Hence by AM-GM we obtain
$$
\frac{MB}{MC} + \frac{NB}{NC} = \frac{M''B''}{M''C} + \frac{N''B''}{N''C} \ge 2 \sqrt{\frac{M''B''}{M''C} \cdot \frac{N''B''}{N''C}} = 2 \cdot \frac{B''E}{CE} = 2 \cdot \frac{|\Delta ABD|}{|\Delta ACD|}
$$' WHERE id = 5388;
UPDATE public.questoes SET solucao = 'Let $AP$ and $BQ$ intersect at $D$ and let $R$ and $S$ be the centres of the circles. Join $PR$, $QS$, $PC$, $QC$ and $DC$ and note the circles touch at $C$. Let $DC$ meet $PQ$ at $E$. Since $PR$ and $QS$ are both perpendicular to $PQ$, $PR$ is parallel to $QS$ and so $\angle PRA = \angle QSC$ which implies that triangles $PRA$ and $QSC$ are similar since both triangles are isosceles. It follows that $\angle PAC = \angle QCB$.
![](attached_image_1.png)
Since $\angle APC = 90^\circ$ (angle in semicircle), we have $90^\circ = \angle PAC + \angle PCA = \angle QCB + \angle PCA$, hence $\angle PCQ = 90^\circ$. Also $\angle CQB = 90^\circ$ (angle in semicircle).
Because $\angle DPC = \angle PCQ = \angle DQC = 90^\circ$, we also have $\angle PDQ = 90^\circ$, which shows that $D$ lies on the circumference of the circle on $AB$ as diameter. In addition $PCQD$ is a rectangle and since the diagonals of a rectangle bisect each other $|EP| = |EQ|$, hence $E$ lies on the radical axis of the two circles and so does $C$. This means that $EC$, and so $DC$ as well, is the common tangent at $C$. This completes the proof.
Let $D$ be the intersection point of the lines $AP$ and $BQ$.
Because $PQ$ is a tangent to the circle with diameter $AC$, the Alternate Segment Theorem gives $\angle QPC = \angle PAC$. The angle $\angle DPC$ is a right angle, because $\angle APC$ is standing on a diameter. Hence, $\angle DPQ = 90^\circ - \angle QPC = 90^\circ - \angle PAC$.
Working with the circle of diameter $CB$ we obtain in a similar way $\angle PQC = \angle QBC$ and $\angle DQP = 90^\circ - \angle PQC = 90^\circ - \angle QBC$.
![](attached_image_2.png)
Considering triangle $DPQ$ we now obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
\angle PDQ &= 180^\circ - (\angle DPQ + \angle DQP) \\
&= 180^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle PAC + 90^\circ - \angle QBC) \\
&= \angle PAC + \angle QBC.
\end{aligned}
$$
Using the angle sum in triangle $DAB$ this implies $2\angle PDQ = 180^\circ$, i.e. $\angle PDQ$ is a right angle. This shows that $D$ is on the circle that has $AB$ as a diameter. This also shows that $PCQD$ is a rectangle, which implies that $\angle PDC = \angle PQC$.
Together with $\angle PQC = \angle QBC$, which we have seen above, we now obtain
$$
\angle PAC + \angle PDC = \angle PAC + \angle QBC = \angle PDQ = 90^\circ.
$$
From triangle $DAC$ we now see that $\angle ACD = 90^\circ$, hence $D$ is on the common tangent to both circles at $C$.
Let $D$ be the intersection point of the lines $AP$ and $BQ$.
The angles $\angle APC$ and $\angle CQB$ are standing on diameters, hence these are right angles. Therefore, the quadrilateral $PCQD$ is cyclic and so
$$
\angle PDC = \angle PQC \quad \text{and} \quad \angle QPC = \angle QDC.
$$
![](attached_image_3.png)
Because $PQ$ is a tangent to the circle with diameter $AC$, the Alternate Segment Theorem gives $\angle QPC = \angle PAC$. Similarly, $\angle PQC = \angle QBC$. Combined with the equations above, we obtain
$$
\angle PDC = \angle QBC \quad \text{and} \quad \angle QDC = \angle PAC
$$
which shows that the triangles $ADC$, $DBC$ and $ABD$ are similar. As a consequence, $\angle DCA = \angle DCB = \angle BDA$. Because $\angle DCA + \angle DCB = 180^\circ$, these three angles are right angles. This implies that $D$ is on the circle with diameter $AB$ and $D$ is on the common tangent to both circles at $C$.
Let $D$ be the intersection point of the line $AP$ and the common tangent at $C$ of the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$. Let $N$ be the intersection point of $PQ$ and $CD$. Because tangents from a point to a circle are of equal length, we have $|NP| = |NC|$ and $|NC| = |NQ|$, hence $N$ is the circumcentre of triangle $PCQ$. As $N$ is on $PQ$, $\angle PCQ$ is a right angle. This implies that $\angle QPC + \angle PQC = 90^\circ$.
The angles $\angle APC$ and $\angle CQB$ are standing on diameters, hence these are right angles. Therefore, $\angle PAC + \angle PCA = \angle QCB + \angle QBC = 90^\circ$.
Because $PQ$ is a tangent to the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$, the Alternate Segment Theorem gives $\angle QPC = \angle PAC$ and $\angle PQC = \angle QBC$. Together with equations obtained earlier, we now have $\angle PAC = \angle QPC = \angle QCB$ and $\angle QBC = \angle PQC = \angle PCA$.
Since $CD$ is perpendicular to $AB$, we also have $\angle PAC + \angle PDC = 90^\circ$ from which we obtain $\angle PDC = \angle PQC$. This equality implies that $D$ is on the circumcircle of triangle $PCQ$. So, $D$ is the intersection point of the common tangent at $C$ and the circumcircle of triangle $PCQ$.
![](attached_image_4.png)
We now let $E$ be the intersection point of the line $BQ$ and the common tangent at $C$ of the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$. We do not know at this stage if $E = D$, as indicated in the diagram.
From the equations obtained above and $\angle QBC + \angle QEC = 90^\circ$ we obtain $\angle QEC = \angle QPC$. This equality implies that $E$ is on the circumcircle of triangle $PCQ$. So, $E$ is the intersection point of the common tangent at $C$ and the circumcircle of triangle $PCQ$, which means that $E = D$. This proves that $AP$, $BQ$ and the common tangent at $C$ are concurrent.
To finish, we just need to note that $\angle ADB = \angle PDC + \angle QEC = 90^\circ$ from the equations obtained before, and this implies that the point $D = E$ is on the circle with diameter $AB$.
Let $D$ be one of the intersection points of the circle with diameter $AB$ and the common tangent at $C$ of the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$. Connect $D$ to $A$ and let $P''$ be the intersection point of this line with the circle that has diameter $AC$. Connect $D$ to $B$ and let $Q''$ be the intersection point of this line with the circle that has diameter $CB$. We wish to prove that $P'' = P$ and $Q'' = Q$. This follows when we have shown that $P''Q''$ is a common tangent to the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$.
![](attached_image_5.png)
To prove this, we first notice that, since $\angle AP''C$, $\angle CQ''B$ and $\angle ADB$ are right angles, we have $\angle DAC = \angle Q''CB$ and $\angle DBC = \angle P''CA$.
Because $CD$ is the radical axis of the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$, the point $D$ has the same power with respect to these two circles, that is $|DP''| \cdot |DA| = |CD|^2 = |DQ''| \cdot |DB|$ and so
$$
\frac{|DA|}{|DB|} = \frac{|DQ''|}{|DP''|}
$$
which implies that triangles $DAB$ and $DQ''P''$ are similar in such a way that
$$
\angle DBC = \angle DP''Q'' \quad \text{and} \quad \angle DAC = \angle DQ''P''.
$$
Using the initially noticed equalities, we see now that
$$
\angle P''CA = \angle DP''Q'' \quad \text{and} \quad \angle Q''CB = \angle DQ''P''.
$$
The converse of the Alternate Segment Theorem implies now that $P''Q''$ is tangent to the circles with diameters $AC$ and $CB$. This shows that $P'' = P$ and $Q'' = Q$ and finishes the proof.' WHERE id = 5389;
