Now, because of (iv), we have $c_{2} \neq 4$. Also, $b_{2} \neq 4$, because otherwise by (*) and (ii) $a_{2}<c_{a_{2}}=b_{2}+1=5$ and there is no number left for $a_{2}$. So we have $a_{2}=4$. Then by (iii) $a_{3} \neq 5$. Also, $b_{2} \neq 5$, because otherwise by (ii) $c_{4}=c_{a_{2}}=b_{2}+1=6$ and there are no numbers left for $c_{2}, c_{3}$. So we have $c_{2}=5$. Using the same arguments we derive $a_{3} \neq 6$ and $b_{2} \neq 6$, hence, $c_{3}=6$. Now, $a_{3} \neq 7$ (by (iii)). Also, $c_{4} \neq 7$, because otherwise by (ii) $7=c_{4}=c_{a_{2}}=b_{2}+1$, and this leads to $b_{2}=6$, which is not true. Hence, $b_{2}=7$. Then $c_{4}=c_{a_{2}}=b_{2}+1=8$.

