We note a period of $5$ in the remainder modulo $19$. As $1010 \equiv 0 \pmod{5}$ we conclude $B = b_{1010} \equiv 1 \pmod{19}$.
Let $b_n$ be the number of ways of covering a $3 \times (2n+1)$ chessboard with one corner square removed with $3n+1$ dominoes. We are interested in the value of $b_{1010}$.
Suppose the corner square is removed from the first column of the $(2n+1) \times 3$ chessboard.
If $n \ge 1$, the dominoes that cover the squares of the last column can be placed in three different ways:
![](attached_image_1.png)
In type 1, the squares of the second last column are already covered. In types 2 and 3, there are two possibilities how the remaining two squares in the second last column can be covered, provided that $n \ge 2$:
![](attached_image_2.png)
When $n=1$ and the bottom square of the first column is removed, type 3b is not possible. Using the obvious $b_0=1$, we see now that $b_1=4$; the four possibilities are obtained from types 1, 2a, 2b and 3a.

Therefore, the contribution to $b_{n+1}$ from types 2b and 3b is equal to the number of possibilities to tile the $(2n+1) \times 3$ chessboard so that the last two columns are not of type 1. As we have seen above, the number of possibilities to tile a $(2n+1) \times 3$ chessboard with the last two columns of type 1 is equal to $b_{n-1}$, hence the contribution to $b_{n+1}$ from types 2b and 3b together is equal to $b_n - b_{n-1}$, and finally
$$
b_{n+1} = 3b_n + b_n - b_{n-1} = 4b_n - b_{n-1}.
$$
Starting with $b_0 = 1$ and $b_1 = 4$ we can now calculate $b_n$ (mod 19):

| n | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
|$b_n$ (mod 19)| 1 | 4 | -4 | -1 | 0 | 1 | 4 |

It follows that $b_n$ (mod 19) repeats with period $5$ and $b_{5k} \equiv 1 \pmod{19}$. In particular $b_{1010} \equiv 1 \pmod{19}$.' WHERE id = 16507;
