It remains to check that the chosen value of $n$ produces non-anchors when multiplied by anchors $m \ge 11$. To prove this, we suppose more generally that either $n \equiv 11 \pmod{20}$ or $n \equiv 19 \pmod{20}$. Since $m \ge 11$ is an anchor, $m$ has at least two digits and the last two digits are odd. Hence $m \in \{11, 13, 15, 17, 19\} \pmod{20}$. Consider the multiplication table (mod 20):

| m \ n | 11 | 19 |
