UPDATE public.questoes SET gabarito = '(a) Para calcular a derivada parcial em $x$, derivamos em $x$, considerando $y$ constante. Assim, temos que
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x, y) = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2 + e^{x^2 y^2}) = 2x + \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2 y^2) e^{x^2 y^2} = 2x + 2xy^2 e^{x^2 y^2}.$$
Analogamente, para calcular a derivada parcial em $y$, derivamos em $y$, considerando $x$ constante. Assim, temos que
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x, y) = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2 + e^{x^2 y^2}) = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2 y^2) e^{x^2 y^2} = 2x^2 y e^{x^2 y^2}.$$

(b)
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x, y, z) = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2 y^2 z^2) \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2) = 2xy^2 z^2 \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2).$$
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x, y, z) = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2 y^2 z^2) \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2) = 2x^2 yz^2 \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2).$$
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}(x, y, z) = \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(x^2 y^2 z^2) \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2) = 2x^2 y^2 z \cos(x^2 y^2 z^2).$$

(c) Temos que
$$f(r, \theta, z) = -\int_{r^2 + \theta^2}^{\theta^2 + z^2} e^{t^2} \, dt.$$
Logo, usando o Teorema Fundamental do Cálculo para uma variável, considerando $\theta$ e $z$ constantes, temos
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial r}(r, \theta, z) = \frac{\partial r} \left( -\int_{r^2 + \theta^2}^{\theta^2 + z^2} e^{t^2} \, dt \right) = -e^{(r^2+\theta^2)^2} \frac{\partial}{\partial r}(r^2 + \theta^2) = -2re^{(r^2+\theta^2)^2}.$$
Também podemos escrever
$$f(r, \theta, z) = \int_{0}^{\theta^2 + z^2} e^{t^2} \, dt + \int_{r^2 + \theta^2}^{0} e^{t^2} \, dt = -\int_{0}^{r^2 + \theta^2} e^{t^2} \, dt + \int_{0}^{\theta^2 + z^2} e^{t^2} \, dt.$$
Assim, usando o Teorema Fundamental do Cálculo na variável $\theta$, considerando $r$ e $z$ constantes, temos
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial \theta}(r, \theta, z) = -e^{(r^2+\theta^2)^2} \cdot 2\theta + e^{(\theta^2+z^2)^2} \cdot 2\theta = -2\theta e^{(r^2+\theta^2)^2} + 2\theta e^{(\theta^2+z^2)^2}.$$
De forma análoga, temos
$$\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}(r, \theta, z) = e^{(\theta^2+z^2)^2} \cdot 2z = 2ze^{(\theta^2+z^2)^2}.$$' WHERE id = 23147;
